# openocd.pin -- the exact upstream revision patches/*.patch is written against
#
# The Makefile's `fetch` target parses the `sha:` line below with a plain
# sed and refuses to continue if the checked-out commit doesn't match it
# exactly. Keep this a single unambiguous "sha:" line.

upstream:    https://github.com/openocd-org/openocd.git
sha:         9ea7f3d647c8ecf6b0f1424002dfc3f4504a162c
pinned-on:   2026-09-17  (repinned master -> v0.12.0 same day)
commit-date: 2023-01-14T23:14:31+03:00
commit-subj: The openocd-0.12.0 release   [tag: v0.12.0]

# WHY THIS REVISION
#
# It is the v0.12.0 release tag, chosen to MATCH THE BENCH: haps-dev runs
# 0.12.0, so this is the bench's own revision rather than an arbitrary one.
# The full reasoning is under "REPINNED" at the bottom of this file.
#
# What makes it a workable base for the patch, verified in the pinned tree:
#
#   - src/flash/nor/max32xxx.c is the closest existing template for a
#     memory-mapped NOR flash driver register layout -- useful to whoever
#     reviews or extends ahb_qspi.c against upstream conventions. (NOTE:
#     max32xxx_qspi.c, the closer match, is a LATER addition and does NOT
#     exist at this tag. Do not go looking for it here.)
#   - driver.h / drivers.c / Makefile.am are in the alphabetically-sorted
#     state the v0.12.0 patch assumes: "ahb_qspi" sorts immediately after
#     "aducm360" and before "ambiqmicro", which is where its hunks land.
#     (The MASTER patch instead assumes am13e230x/artery as the following
#     entry -- drivers that did not exist in 2023. That is the concrete
#     reason the two patches are not interchangeable.)
#
# No CVE or regression is being chased by this pin. It exists so the patch has
# one fixed, re-diffable base -- re-running `fetch` against a moving "master"
# would eventually drift the context lines out from under it and turn a clean
# insertion into a manual merge.
#
# BUMPING THE PIN
#
# 1. Re-derive the patch against the new commit (the three hunks are each
#    one line; regenerating by hand is faster than trusting a 3-way merge
#    silently getting it wrong).
# 2. Confirm aducm360/am13* are still adjacent to where ahb_qspi lands in
#    all three files -- if upstream renamed or resorted anything nearby,
#    the patch context needs updating, not just the sha here.
# 3. Update sha / pinned-on / commit-date / commit-subj together, in one
#    commit, so a `git log` on this file is a legible history of what was
#    validated when.

# REPINNED 2026-09-17: master -> v0.12.0.
#
# haps-dev runs OpenOCD 0.12.0 (/usr/local/bin/openocd, built from source, not a
# package). Building master there would put a DIFFERENT OpenOCD version on the
# bench than every existing config and script has been validated against, for no
# gain -- the driver needs nothing master added.
#
# Verified 2026-09-17 against v0.12.0: configure OK, `make -j8` OK, ZERO warnings
# in ahb_qspi.c's translation unit, `strings src/openocd` contains "ahb_qspi",
# and `flash bank ... ahb_qspi` is accepted (it fails on the missing target, not
# on an unknown driver). The driver's .erase/.write signatures are identical in
# v0.12.0 and master, which is why no source change was needed.
#
# TWO PATCHES EXIST -- pick the one matching your pin:
#   patches/0001-register-ahb_qspi-v0.12.0.patch   <- for THIS pin. 2 files:
#       in 0.12.0 the `extern` declarations live in drivers.c, so Makefile.am
#       gets the source line and drivers.c gets BOTH the extern and the array
#       entry.
#   patches/0001-register-ahb_qspi.patch           <- for master. 3 files:
#       master moved the externs out into driver.h.
#   The master patch does NOT apply to v0.12.0 -- its context lines reference
#   drivers (am13e230x, artery) that did not exist in 2023.
